BM3501.description:
The purpose of this course is to prepare you for real world marketing challenges involving complex decisions that carry financial consequences for the firm. This is an advanced course for undergraduate students specializing in Marketing. Taken in the final semester of your final year, this course serves to integrate all the concepts and frameworks that you have learned from prior marketing courses. You will address critical decision issues involved in marketing planning at a strategic level, including segmentation and positioning, product development, new market entry, and management of the marketing budget.
In order to gear you up for the role of a Marketing Manager (a position which many of you are aiming - and likely - to hold within five years of graduation), this course provides an experiential learning environment in which you will gain not only an appreciation but also a personal feel for the tasks of strategic marketing planning and decision making. Within this environment, you will learn to become an effective marketing decision maker, one who is capable of minimizing (though not completely removing) much of the uncertainties surrounding marketing decisions through disciplined analysis and prudent judgment. In this course, you do not just read about marketing strategy formulation;
you practice it! And you do not just talk strategy recommendation; you execute it (and be accountable for the results of it)!

CV2019.description:
This course aims to: 

i) Provide you with the knowledge of the fundamental principles of linear algebra and basic matrix operations; 

ii) Equip you with the ability to apply computational methods to obtain solutions to problems involving: roots of equation; interpolations; differentiation and integration; simple ordinary differential equation and partialdifferential equations.

EE4273.description:
Digital controllers are used in a wide variety of systems ranging from disk drives to aircrafts. Thus, it is very important to be well-versed in the analysis and design of digital control systems. The course aims to equip you with the knowledge of:
Various issues related to digital control systems such as effects of sampling and quantization, discrete time signals and models;
Design and implementation of digital controllers, implementation issues such as sampling and quantization arising from practical digital controllers.

HG2097.description:
The Course introduces fundamental topics in Etymology and Historical Onomastics. 

Key subjects covered include:

- basic elements of General Etymology as a Reconstruction Science in Linguistics;

- elementary elements of the various branches of Etymological Sciences, Lexicography, Onomastics, and Toponomastics;

- comparative readings and exercises ? at an elementary level ? about the subject of the Course, in order that the students can practice directly with Etymological Sciences;

- basic elements of Historical Linguistics and Etymology in Historical Onomastics and Historical Toponomastics (Toponymy, Hydronymy, and Oronymy); 

- direct and experimental application ? at a general, elementary level ? of Historical Phonetics and Historical Laws, case-by-case, to specific cases and samples, mainly focused on some languages of the South-East Asian context; 

- discussion and debate with students inherent in the topic of the Course

